---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { AGENT_MIN_CONTEXT, AGENT_MIN_RESPONDED_DAYS, AGENT_WINDOW_DAYS } from "../../pipeline/agent-ready";
import { MIN_TESTED_DAYS, RANKINGS, RANKING_WINDOW_DAYS } from "../../pipeline/rankings";
import { UNREACHABLE_AFTER } from "../../pipeline/reachability";
import { HIGH_IMPACT_THRESHOLD } from "../../pipeline/scoring";
import { SITE } from "../../site.config";
---
<Base title="Methodology" description="How FreeTier Radar finds free models, tests them, labels them, and handles sponsors.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">What we check, how often, what the labels mean, and what we will never do.</p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>
      We read each provider's own model list through its official API, every 6 hours. We prefer
      official APIs and machine-readable files over web pages, and we never scrape where
      automated access is not allowed.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>OpenRouter:</strong> models with the <code>:free</code> suffix. Its free voices aren't in any OpenRouter list, so we keep those by hand (labelled <em>Hand-listed</em>) and the daily test shows whether they still work.</li>
      <li><strong>Groq:</strong> every active chat, text-to-speech (Orpheus) and speech-to-text (Whisper) model. All are usable on the free plan.</li>
      <li><strong>Google AI Studio:</strong> every Gemini chat, text-to-speech and transcription model. The list does not say which are on the free tier; the daily test with a free-tier key does.</li>
      <li><strong>NVIDIA:</strong> every hosted chat model, all labelled evaluation-only.</li>
      <li><strong>Kilo Code gateway</strong> and <strong>LLM7.io</strong>: their free models, which work without an account or key. We read their public model lists and test them anonymously. LLM7's terms rule out production use.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Image generation and embedding models are not in the directory yet.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Free credits and quotas</strong> (trial credit, monthly credit, daily allowances) are kept by hand on the
      <a href="/offers/">Free credits</a> page, each with the date we last checked it. Once a week we fetch each
      provider's source page and compare it with last week's; when it changes, we recheck the record.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      Every run is compared with the last known state. We record a change only when a tracked field
      changes: price, context window, input types, tool calling, rate limits, usage terms,
      licence, or card/account requirements.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A model that disappears is first marked <em>missing</em>. It is only reported as removed after being missing for at least 12 hours, because free capacity often flickers.</li>
      <li>If a provider's list cannot be fetched, nothing about that provider changes until it can.</li>
      <li>The first check of a provider sets the baseline and produces no "new" entries.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The daily test</h2>
    <p>
      Once a day we send each free model one tiny request, using our own free-tier accounts:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chat:</strong> “Reply with the single word: OK”, 16 tokens max.</li>
      <li><strong>Text-to-speech:</strong> speak the word “OK”; it passes when audio comes back.</li>
      <li><strong>Speech-to-text:</strong> transcribe an 18-second narration; it passes when the transcript matches.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The result is shown as “Responded at 06:10 UTC”. It proves the model answered at that moment, not that it is
      fast or available all day. A rate-limit answer is not recorded as a failure. “Agent apps only” means the
      provider refused a direct request because it only serves that model through the coding-agent apps it lists.
    </p>
    <p>
      A model its provider lists, but that answers “not found” on {UNREACHABLE_AFTER} daily tests in a row, is
      left out of the directory and marked <em>Listed but not callable</em> on its page and provider page. It
      returns as soon as it answers again.
    </p>

    <h2>Rankings</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/rankings/">rankings</a> use only what we measure: how often a model answered the daily test over
      the last {RANKING_WINDOW_DAYS} days (rate-limited days don't count), how fast, whether it fits the job, and,
      where a model has one, its <a href="#benchmarks">LMArena rating</a>. Evaluation-only models are left out.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {RANKINGS.map((r) => <li><strong>{r.title}:</strong> {r.order}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="sub">The reliability ranking needs at least {MIN_TESTED_DAYS} tested days per model.</p>

    <h2 id="benchmarks">Benchmark ratings</h2>
    <p>
      Chat models show their <strong>LMArena</strong> ratings (Text, WebDev, Vision, and the Agent leaderboard's rank)
      from the <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/lmarena-ai/leaderboard-dataset" rel="noopener">LMArena leaderboard dataset</a>,
      which is published under CC-BY-4.0. We refresh it weekly. Ratings come from people comparing two anonymous
      answers, so they measure preference, not correctness.
    </p>
    <p>
      Providers and LMArena name models differently. A model is linked automatically when the names match after
      removing provider prefixes and suffixes like <code>:free</code>, <code>-it</code> or <code>-instruct</code>.
      Near matches, such as a Gemini model and its "high" reasoning variant, are only shown after we've checked them
      by hand. Many small or new free models have no rating yet.
    </p>

    <h2>Weekly roundup</h2>
    <p>
      Every Monday the week's changes are collected into a <a href="/roundups/">roundup</a>. A free model writes a
      two- or three-sentence intro from a list of the week's facts and is told not to add anything; every entry
      below it comes straight from the change feed. We read and edit each roundup before it's published.
    </p>

    <h2>Labels</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>🤖 Agent-ready</strong>: tool calling, at least {AGENT_MIN_CONTEXT / 1000}k context, answered on {AGENT_MIN_RESPONDED_DAYS} of the last {AGENT_WINDOW_DAYS} test days, and not evaluation-only. An asterisk means the free request limit is low and shared across the provider's free models; coding agents send one request per tool step.</li>
      <li><strong>⚠ Eval only</strong>: the provider's terms allow testing and evaluation, not production use.</li>
      <li><strong>No card</strong>: no payment card needed to use the free tier. <strong>No signup</strong>: works without an account.</li>
      <li><strong>👁 Logs prompts / May use your prompts</strong>: the provider says it logs prompts, or may use them to train or improve its models or service. Kilo publishes this per model. Don't send personal or confidential data to these.</li>
      <li><strong>Unknown</strong> is a real value. If a provider does not publish something, we do not guess.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Impact score</h2>
    <p>
      Each change gets a score from 0 to 100, used only for sorting and for flagging changes worth
      a closer look (score {HIGH_IMPACT_THRESHOLD} or higher). New free models start at 50,
      removals at 45, and changes to price, terms or limits at 55. Tool calling, a context window of
      128k or more, a context window of 1M or more, and image input each add points;
      evaluation-only terms subtract 20. It is not an estimate of value.
    </p>

    <h2>Sponsors</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE.name} has one sponsor slot, always labelled “Sponsor”. A sponsor never affects
      rankings, labels, test results or change text, and a sponsor's own products are shown with the same
      honest labels as everything else. No tracking scripts, no ad networks.
    </p>

    <h2>Open data</h2>
    <p>
      All data lives in a public <a href={SITE.repo}>GitHub repository</a>, and every change is a commit.
      You can also use the <a href="/api/resources.json">JSON feed</a> and <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
